p(40,4,"The man flung his tattered rug over the horse, turned full round",
 "Sam says that the masters ask too much money, and the fares are too low. He and the horses must work too long, and he has no Sunday and no warm coat.",
 "The man threw his torn rug over the horse and turned right round to the Governor. In a voice that sounded almost hopeless he said that if the police had any business with the matter, it ought to be with the masters who charge so much, or with the fares that are fixed so low. A man has to pay eighteen shillings a day for a cab and two horses, as many of them must in the busy time, and he must make that money before he earns a penny for himself. Sam says that is more than hard work. He says that nine shillings a day must be got out of each horse before a man begins to get his own living. He says the Governor knows it is true. If the horses do not work, they must starve, and Sam and his children have known what that is. He has six children, and only one earns anything. He is on the stand fourteen or sixteen hours a day, and he has not had a Sunday for these ten or twelve weeks. He says that Skinner never gives a day if he can help it. He wants a warm coat and a mackintosh, but with so many to feed, how can he get them? He had to give his clock to a man for money a week ago, to pay Skinner, and he will never see it again.",
 ["tattered","desperate","shillings","earn","starve","mackintosh","pledge"])
s(40,"I wish some of ’em had got to sit on my box",
 "I wish some of them had to sit on my box sixteen hours a day.",
 "'wish some of them had got to sit' uses the past tense after 'wish' for something that is not true now; 'had got to' means 'had to'.")
